# Dynamics, tempo and texture read from a score: the marks as the Italian words they stand for, the
# words for speed, where a part gets louder or softer, and how many parts sound at once and how they
# fit, named monophonic, homophonic and polyphonic, with Haydn's Surprise Symphony (1791), Ode to Joy
# and Frère Jacques at the waterfall gorge.
lesson music-dynamics-and-texture v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=5 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Dynamics and texture"
  goal "Read a score along each part for how loud and how fast it goes, and down the page for its texture."

  look {
    scene 33x30 {
      column score space=0 align=start at=canvas(1, 1) {
        notes flute clef=treble notes=[E4, E4, F4, G4, G4, F4, E4, D4] values=[] meter=4 spaced=true
        dynamics marks marks=["p", "cresc", "f"] words=true ring=-1
        notes cello clef=bass notes=[C3, G2] values=[4, 4] meter=4 spaced=true
      }
    }
    say """
      By the waterfall the band reads from a score, one staff for each part. The marks under a part
      are short for Italian words: p is piano, soft, f is forte, loud, and m is mezzo, half, so mf
      is moderately loud. A hairpin that opens is a crescendo, getting gradually louder; one that
      closes is a diminuendo, getting gradually softer. Sfz, sforzando, pushes one note hard, and
      fp, forte-piano, is loud then soft at once. Words over the score give the speed, from largo,
      very slow, through andante, a walking pace, and allegro, fast, to presto, very fast;
      ritardando slows down and a tempo goes back to the speed before. The texture is how the parts
      sound together. One tune, even doubled, is monophonic. A tune with an accompaniment, like the
      flute here over the cello's long notes, is homophonic. Two tunes at once, as in a round, is
      polyphonic.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.dyn-words k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level easy {
      worked music.dyn-end-louder k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice music.dyn-end-louder count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    show music.tempo-words k=4 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    practice music.texture-kind count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice music.texture-round count=1 seed=4 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice music.dyn-surprise count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    show music.texture-round b=3 easy-b=2 hard-b=4
    level hard {
      show music.dyn-claim q=1 r=1
    }
    practice music.gorge-parts-count count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.dyn-claim q=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.dyn-bridge-paths v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Read along a part for how loud and how fast; read down the score for how the parts fit together."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a score shows several things at once: along each staff, how loud and how fast
    the part plays and where that changes, written in Italian words and their short marks, and down
    the page, how the parts fit together, named monophonic (one tune, even doubled), homophonic (a
    tune and an accompaniment) and polyphonic (two tunes at once, as in a round). The wrong ideas to
    watch for are reading a crescendo as loud rather than as getting louder from wherever the music
    is, taking a sudden mark such as sfz or fp for a gradual one, and calling two instruments on the
    same tune two tunes. In the working, look for the child saying the Italian word for a mark before
    choosing, and running a finger straight down the page to find what sounds together. The two-star
    question is the claim that a hairpin means loud, when this one ends at mp; the three-star question
    counts the ways a line of marks can climb and fall one step at a time, which needs every way
    written as ups and downs and checked against the ends of the ladder. A child who is stuck can sing
    a phrase getting louder and then softer, then slower, and go back to the way in; stay on this
    lesson another day if parts on a score are still read one after another rather than together. Off
    the page, listen to the slow movement of Haydn's Surprise Symphony and wait for the chord, then
    listen to a round and count the voices.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the word to read is mezzo forte, reading louder or softer by the end is worked once in the teacher's pen before a tune with two hairpins, the speed to name is andante, the textures are a doubled tune and an accompaniment, the round is read on beats 1 and 2, Haydn's marks are one step apart, the parts are counted where only rests stand in the way, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star climbs from p in three bars."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the word to read is forte-piano, loud then soft at once, the tunes to read have a loud or soft middle that is a trap, the speed word is a tempo after a ritardando, the textures include a second tune, the round is read where a long note is still sounding, Haydn's chord is played one step softer, a claim that two players make two tunes is judged before the two-star, rests are scattered through three parts, and the three-star returns to f without passing ff."
  }
}
